\centering
\footnotesize
\captionof{table}{Neural probe pairwise AUC using frozen-encoder MLP. Each cell reports AUC for classifying depth $d$ vs.\ $d{+}1$ text. $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}$: maximum depth where AUC exceeds $\theta = 0.60$.}
\label{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{2v3 $\auc$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B  & 0.6159 & $[0.610,\,0.622]$ & 3 \\
Pythia-1.4B & 0.5963 & $[0.589,\,0.603]$ & 2 \\
OLMo-1B    & 0.5892 & $[0.582,\,0.597]$ & 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{minipage}

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Cross-Domain Variation}
\label{sec:cross_domain}

The preceding experiments use C4 (general web text).
To test whether $\Kempirical$ reflects domain structure or model identity, we evaluate on arXiv abstracts ($n{=}2{,}200$ per model) and Wikipedia ($n{=}5{,}000$ per model) across six model configurations spanning four architecture families (Table~\ref{tab:domain_kstar}).

\paragraph{arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$): five-model cross-validation.}
All five domain-competent models yield $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv, with boundary-pair $\auc$ (2v3) ranging from 0.6215 to 0.6545, all exceeding $\theta$.
The elevated signal reflects higher per-generation distributional shift (1v2 JSD $= 0.077$ vs.\ ${\sim}0.01$ for C4): formulaic features (LaTeX formatting, citation patterns, mathematical notation) degrade slowly under autoregressive generation and supply persistent depth signal across four architecture families and 1.4B--8B parameters.
Removing LaTeX markup reduces 2v3 AUC by ${\sim}$1.8\,pp but preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ (CI lower $= 0.610 > \theta$; Table~\ref{tab:latex_ablation}); content-level properties dominate.
OLMo-1B constitutes a capacity failure rather than a counter-example; its arXiv generations are entirely out-of-domain (0v1 JSD $= 0.33$, near-total information loss at the first step), yielding $\Kempirical{=}1$.
Mistral-7B provides the strongest domain-dependence evidence: $\Kempirical{=}1$ on C4 (Section~\ref{sec:boundary_cases}) but $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv: domain structure alone can shift $\Kempirical$ by two levels within the same model.

\paragraph{Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$): single-generation saturation.}
With scorer confound controlled (Pythia-1.4B for Qwen generators; Qwen-1.5B, itself cross-family, for all others), all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia: 1v2 $\auc$ ranges from 0.499 (OLMo) to 0.595 (LLaMA), all below $\theta$.
Wikipedia represents the simplest domain tested; its encyclopedic regularity provides minimal structural signal beyond the 0v1 boundary, and a single generation pass suffices for complete saturation.
A same-family scorer confound inflates the apparent $\Kempirical$ for Qwen generators: Qwen-1.5B scoring yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc \approx 0.61$--$0.62$), but cross-family scoring reduces 1v2 by 2.4\,pp to below $\theta$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}).
This +1 depth bias under same-family scoring is specific to Qwen generators on Wikipedia and does not affect C4 results (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).

\paragraph{Code ($\Kempirical{=}1$--$2$): cross-modality confirmation.}
Python functions from CodeSearchNet~\cite{husain2019codesearchnet} ($n{=}5{,}000$) yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ for Qwen2.5-1.5B (1v2 $\auc = 0.738$; Table~\ref{tab:code_domain_qwen}), LLaMA-3.1-8B ($\auc = 0.739$), and Mistral-7B-v0.3 ($\auc = 0.615$, CI lower $= 0.603$; Table~\ref{tab:code_crossmodel_kstar}), but $\Kempirical{=}1$ for capacity-limited Pythia-1.4B ($\auc = 0.598$, CI upper $= 0.603$).
Code matches C4 despite a different modality, with the same sharp 2v3 collapse (JSD ratio $11.7$--$13.5\times$).
Cross-family scorer validation confirms Code $\Kempirical{=}2$ is text-intrinsic ($\Delta \leq 1.9$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:code_domain}).

\paragraph{Domain as the primary factor.}
With the scorer confound controlled, the domain ordering is: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $\geq$ Code ($\Kempirical{=}1$--$2$) $\geq$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).
Scale (1B--14B) and architecture do not alter the C4/arXiv/Wikipedia ordering; code shows model-dependent variation at the boundary, but domain structural complexity remains the primary determinant of $\Kempirical$.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model collapse and recursive degradation.}
Iterative training on model outputs causes tail-distribution degradation~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, alemohammad2024selfconsuming, dohmatob2024model, dohmatob2025strong}, diversity contraction~\cite{guo2024curious, padmakumar2024writing, briesch2023selfconsuming}, and predictable convergence~\cite{bertrand2024stability, schaeffer2025position, kazdan2025collapse, gerstgrasser2024model, shi2025collapse}.
\citet{gambetta2024surplexity} propose surplexity, a single surprisal-based collapse indicator; our 15-dimensional feature set captures richer distributional structure and enables cross-model transfer.
\citet{amin2025escaping} show that weak-data curation can mitigate collapse, connecting to our filtering evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}).
Our prompt-chain operates in the replace regime~\cite{kazdan2025collapse}; $\Kempirical = 2$ is therefore a conservative lower bound.
This literature establishes convergence but provides no per-text depth measurement, does not predict domain dependence of saturation (Wikipedia depth 1 vs.\ arXiv depth 3), and does not characterize the prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain gap ($\Kempirical{=}2$ vs.\ $7$).

\paragraph{Beyond binary detection.}
Binary detectors~\cite{gehrmann2019gltr, mitchell2023detectgpt, bao2024fast, yang2024dnagpt, hans2024binoculars, verma2024ghostbuster, basani2026diveye, zhang2024minkpp} achieve strong human-vs.-machine separation but produce no depth estimate; surveys~\cite{wu2023survey, tang2024science} consolidate this landscape but frame detection as binary.
Recent work extends beyond binary labels to collaboration degree~\cite{cheng2025beyond}, contamination hierarchies~\cite{mehta2025beyond}, and multigenerator benchmarks~\cite{wang2024semeval}; robustness concerns arise under paraphrasing~\cite{sadasivan2023detect, krishna2023paraphrasing} and cross-mode transfer~\cite{hao2025rewrite}; CoDeC~\cite{zawalski2026codec} addresses contamination via membership inference.
None estimates recursive generation depth.
Watermarking~\cite{kirchenbauer2023watermark} embeds detectable signals at generation time but does not apply to text already in the wild.

\paragraph{Data selection and curation.}
DSIR~\cite{xie2023dsir}, D4~\cite{tirumala2023d4}, DoReMi~\cite{xie2023doremi}, and FineWeb~\cite{penedo2024fineweb} address corpus quality for large web corpora such as C4~\cite{raffel2020t5} through domain-aware filtering and deduplication but are depth-agnostic; Drayson et al.~\cite{drayson2025} combined binary detection with importance resampling, a special case when $\Kstar = 1$.
\citet{oren2024proving} address contamination detection in pre-training data; our method extends this to depth-granular resolution.

\paragraph{Ordinal classification and probing.}
Our surprisal features build on information-theoretic processing cost~\cite{hale2001surprisal, levy2008expectation}, repurposed to track generational degradation.
Our OBD classifier follows Frank \& Hall's~\cite{frank2001ordinal} binary decomposition; CORAL~\cite{cao2020coral} provides a rank-consistent neural alternative.
Our neural probe extends probing~\cite{alain2017understanding, belinkov2022probing} to the discrimination boundary.

